          "guid": "mryazrbnnezjl1qs",
          "message": {
            "text": "git show cannot see LFS content; git cat-file --filters can.\n\ngit show <ref>:<path> reads the object database, and for an LFS-tracked file that object is the pointer. git cat-file --filters runs the same content through git's configured smudge filters, which is exactly what materializes the real bytes — no dependency on the git lfs porcelain being on PATH, and it works for any filter a repo configures, not just LFS.\n\nApplied only when the first read returns a pointer, so an ordinary file keeps the cheaper path and is never put through a filter that might rewrite it (CRLF conversion, for instance).\n\nFail-soft matters here. In a partial clone, or with LFS not installed, the smudge is a no-op and hands the pointer straight back. Returning it would feed pointer text to an <img> and render a corrupt image with no explanation — so the second pointer check returns null instead, and the reviewer sees a missing image, which is at least honest.\n\nVerified against the real repo: git show yields 130 bytes of pointer, git cat-file --filters yields 232246 bytes starting with the PNG signature — matching the size the pointer declares.\n\nSee also the detection half (0).",
            "markdown": "`git show` cannot see LFS content; `git cat-file --filters` can.\n\n`git show <ref>:<path>` reads the object database, and for an LFS-tracked file that object *is* the pointer. `git cat-file --filters` runs the same content through git's configured smudge filters, which is exactly what materializes the real bytes — no dependency on the `git lfs` porcelain being on PATH, and it works for any filter a repo configures, not just LFS.\n\nApplied only when the first read returns a pointer, so an ordinary file keeps the cheaper path and is never put through a filter that might rewrite it (CRLF conversion, for instance).\n\n**Fail-soft matters here.** In a partial clone, or with LFS not installed, the smudge is a no-op and hands the pointer straight back. Returning it would feed pointer text to an `<img>` and render a corrupt image with no explanation — so the second pointer check returns null instead, and the reviewer sees a missing image, which is at least honest.\n\nVerified against the real repo: `git show` yields 130 bytes of pointer, `git cat-file --filters` yields 232246 bytes starting with the PNG signature — matching the `size` the pointer declares.\n\nSee also [the detection half](0)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/git/image.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 53,
                  "endLine": 75,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "/** Read a file at a specific git ref. Returns null if the file doesn't exist at that ref. */\nfunction gitShowFile(ref: string, filePath: string, repoRoot: string): Buffer | null {\n  const spec = ref === ':' ? `:${filePath}` : `${ref}:${filePath}`;\n  const result = spawnSync('git', ['show', spec], { cwd: repoRoot, maxBuffer: 50 * 1024 * 1024, env: scrubbedGitEnv() });\n  if (result.status !== 0 || result.stdout.length === 0) return null;\n  // `git show` reads the object database, which for an LFS-tracked file holds\n  // the pointer, not the image. `git cat-file --filters` runs the same content\n  // through git's smudge filters, which is what materializes the real bytes.\n  // Only reached for an actual pointer, so a normal file keeps the cheaper path\n  // and is never put through a filter that might rewrite it.\n  if (isLfsPointer(result.stdout)) {\n    const smudged = spawnSync('git', ['cat-file', '--filters', spec], { cwd: repoRoot, maxBuffer: 50 * 1024 * 1024, env: scrubbedGitEnv() });\n    // Fail soft: without the LFS object available (a partial clone, or LFS not\n    // installed) the smudge is a no-op that hands back the pointer again. Better\n    // a missing image than a corrupt one.\n    if (smudged.status === 0 && smudged.stdout.length > 0 && !isLfsPointer(smudged.stdout)) {\n      return smudged.stdout;\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n  return result.stdout;\n}\n"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
